% CHALLENGES
\sectiontitle{Challenges/Roadblocks (7 pts)}

\vspace{0.05cm}



\vspace{0.18cm}

\answerbox{13.7cm}{One of the main challenges during the development of STUDYWISE was working with frontend technologies such as React, Vite and Tailwind CSS. Since these technologies are not covered in much detail in our university syllabus, understanding their structure and connecting the frontend with the C++ backend required additional learning and practice. We gradually became more familiar with these tools while developing the different pages and integrating them with the existing APIs.\\

Another difficulty was configuring the C++ backend and connecting it with the frontend. Setting up the Crow framework, managing the CMake configuration and resolving compiler-related issues took considerable effort. We also faced some compatibility problems with different compiler versions. These issues were addressed by using a suitable C++17 compiler, testing the backend separately and checking the API communication before integrating the changes into the application.\\

Implementing the data structures and algorithms correctly was also challenging. The custom Max Heap required careful handling of insertion, swapping and extraction operations to maintain the correct priority order. Similarly, the searching and sorting algorithms had to produce consistent results for different inputs. Developing the weighted priority calculation also required attention because several factors, including exam urgency, remaining syllabus, difficulty, academic importance and subject workload, contribute to the final score.\\

During Stage 6, handling PDF uploads introduced additional challenges. The backend needed to validate uploaded files, maintain their association with subjects and topics, store them safely in the local uploads directory and preserve their metadata in JSON files. We also had to handle file replacement, deletion and missing-file situations carefully.\\

In the final stage, creating visualizations that correctly represented the actual C++ algorithms required additional effort. Making the frontend display the correct steps returned by the backend and ensuring that all existing modules continued to work together involved further testing and debugging. We addressed these issues by developing the project module by module and carrying out regular build checks, API testing and integration testing.}

